  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 151 PM CST Thu Nov 26 2026\n\nCurrent surface analysis places ridge over Lake Superior into\nsouthern Ontario, with low over eastern Alberta. Upper level \nanalysis places low just off the British Colombia coast with \nnotable short wave ejecting off to the southeast, while ridge \nis noted over the western end of the northern plains. Over our \narea, quite weather with mild temperatures continue with the \nnoted upper ridge.\n\nFor tonight, surface low will quickly move east through\nSaskatchewan, nudging into Manitoba by daybreak, while upper\nlevel short wave takes a similar path quickly ejecting to the\neast over the Rockies in the evening, closing up and deepening\nover Saskatchewan late tonight. A frontal boundary will slide\ninto the state later tonight, while along and ahead of the\nboundary, light rain chances (20 to 30 percent) develop and push\nwest to east in conjunction with a short wave swinging around\nthe aforementioned upper low. While precipitation should be\nmainly rain, there is a low potential for a little light\nfreezing rain over the far southern James River Valley towards\nearly morning as sounding profiles indicate freezing surface\ntemperatures will be possible while temperatures remain mild\naloft. \n\nOn Friday, precipitation chances will continue to progress east\nin the early morning. Thereafter, a mild day is on tap with\ntemperatures climbing to around 10 to 15 degrees above average.\nWesterly winds behind the front will be on the increase as \nsurface low meanders east, sliding over Lake Winnipeg by early \nevening. A secondary front drops through later Friday/Friday\nnight which will start the true push of cooler air into the\narea.\n\nOn Saturday, stacked low gradually meanders eastward over the \nManitoba/Ontario border, resulting in another windy day over our\narea, with cooler temperatures. Another push of cold air slides\nsouthward Saturday afternoon into Saturday night. This, combined\nwith a notable wave swinging southeast of the low, will bring \nlow chances for snow to the northern part of our area.\n\nCooler air will be well settled into the area on Sunday, with a\nlittle bit of warming expected going into the upcoming work\nweek. A generally fast westerly flow pattern sets up for the\nfirst half of the upcoming week, with a variety of waves passing\nthrough bringing occasional low chances for light snow/rain.",
